+### Control sections and code areas in Scimitar forge archives
+
+- **Symptom:** `the shader split found no shaders in main or the RomFS` on a RomFS of `.forge` files (`AC2/DataNX*.forge`, `scimitar` at +0), `.pck` Wwise banks and `.bk2` movies; the split's log reports about 60 control sections in `main` with no code area.
+- **Cause:** Ubisoft's Anvil engine keeps its data in Scimitar `.forge` files: a header, chained tables of 16-byte index entries {offset, id, size} and 0xbc-byte name records, then the entries, each a 0x1b8-byte `FILEDATA` header (which the index's size leaves out) and compressed blocks (magic `33 AA FB 57 99 FA 04 10`, a table of {raw, stored} u16 sizes, then a u32 checksum and the stored bytes per chunk). The chunks are Oodle Leviathan (`8C 0C`, block type 6, version 31 files) or Mermaid (`8C 0A`, type 4, version 25), stored as they are when the sizes match. The loose scan reads them compressed and finds nothing. In the decoded material templates each NVN shader is a 0x880-byte control section with its code area right after it.
+- **Fix:** `dm/gfx/tools/forge.py` reads the tables, the entries' blocks and the shaders in a decoded block; `shader_split.py` (`forge_jobs`, `forge_shaders`) decodes every entry with the game's own `OodleLZ_Decompress` in the Mac host's tool mode, as for Trinity packs, and leaves the `.forge` files out of the loose scan. `profile_guess.py` turns the Oodle decoder on for a RomFS with `.forge` files (`oodle_forge`; the survey counts the `Scimitar forge` magic). The templates name no vertex / pixel pairs, so the tables have none. `main`'s control sections stay left out, as before. `test_forge.py` covers the tables, stored and decoded chunks, the header the index leaves out, the shader pairs and damaged files.
+- **Found on:** Assassin's Creed: The Ezio Collection 1.3 (no profile yet): AC II's 27 forge files (in the RomFS) hold 224,124 shaders, 20,417 different (1,514 vertex, 18,903 pixel); Brotherhood's 29 (the first DLC, `ACB/`) hold 26,123 different; 57,834 entries with blocks across both; 46,540 shaders in the split, which takes about 40 s with 8 workers. The AC II audio DLC holds only sound banks.
+
 ### SPK shader package in an MT Framework ARC
 
 - **How it is stored:** the engine's one shader package, `sc\NX_64\root` (`SPK\0`, type hash `0x02358e1a`) in `nativeNXx64/sa/NX_64/root.arc` ([01](../agents/01-extract.md)). After the package's tables every shader is a code area followed directly by its control section, back to back to the end of the file: 230 vertex shaders, then 230 pixel shaders.
@@ -539,6 +546,7 @@ Finding, keying and translating shaders (`dm/gfx/tools/shader_split.py`, `dmsh_b
 - **Cause:** a game that builds one NVN program per GLSLC output and combines vertex and pixel programs per draw with `BindProgram` stage bits has its real pairs only at draw time, so the offline build cannot know which pixel shaders a vertex shader must feed.
 - **Fix:** the composite-program path ([Programs bound per stage](rendering.md#programs-bound-per-stage)) does all the pairing at replay. When every pixel shader input is a plain `float4` at fixed locations, `shader_split.py` pairs every vertex shader with a *cover*: the fewest pixel shaders whose inputs together are every input any pixel shader reads. `vs_pad` then gives each vertex shader all of them, and any vertex / pixel combination links.
